      "id": 1,
      "text": "Cincinnati, Arkansas, was renamed in 1857 to honor Roman statesman Lucius Quinctius Cincinnatus.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/cincinnati_arkansas",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Before 1857, the community was first called Buzzard Roost, then Silvia, reflecting its early frontier roots.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cincinnati%2C_Arkansas",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In the 19th century, Cincinnati emerged as a key trade hub on the frontier adjacent to Indian Territory.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/cincinnati_arkansas",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "By the late 1880s, Cincinnati hosted general stores, a wagon factory, grain mills, tanneries, and blacksmith shops, generating over $224,000 in annual goods.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/cincinnati_arkansas",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The town sits on the west bank of Cincinnati Creek, a tributary that feeds the Springfield Plateau aquifer and can flood during heavy rains.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        14,
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cincinnati%2C_Arkansas",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Cincinnati lies at about 1,070 feet on a dissected plateau, surrounded by rolling hills and narrow valleys shaped by ancient erosion.",
